    firstAidGuide: {
      breadcrumb: "Extended First-Aid Guide",
      eyebrow: "Safety Guide",
      title: "Extended First-Aid Guide for a Snakebite",
      lead: "What to do — and what not to do — in the first minutes after a snakebite. A practical, step-by-step guide that could save a life.",
      emergency: "For any snakebite: call emergency services immediately and go to the nearest hospital. This guide is for awareness and is not a substitute for urgent medical care.",
      introA: "A snakebite is a frightening situation, but what you do in the first minutes can make a big difference. The reassuring news is that fatalities are very rare in Israel when the victim reaches hospital in time, and an effective antivenom is available for the most dangerous species.",
      introB: "The most important rule to remember: calm and speed together. Calm slows the venom's spread, and speed gets you to treatment. As for the old folk measures, many of them do more harm than good, and we'll explain them in detail.",
      s1title: "The first minute: what to do immediately",
      s1: "Move a safe distance from the snake to avoid a second bite, without trying to kill or catch it. Sit or lie down in a safe place and try to stay as calm as possible; fear and running speed up the heart rate and the venom's spread. Call emergency services immediately or ask someone nearby to do so, and note the time of the bite if you can.",
      s2title: "The correct steps, one by one",
      step1: "Call for help immediately, and give them your location and the time of the bite.",
      step2: "Keep the victim calm and still, preferably lying down or sitting with minimal movement.",
      step3: "Remove rings, bracelets, watches, and anything tight near the bite before the swelling worsens.",
      step4: "Gently support the affected limb in a comfortable position, keeping it near heart level without raising it high or lowering it much.",
      step5: "Monitor the victim (breathing and consciousness) until help arrives, and get them to hospital without delay.",
      s3title: "What you must never do",
      dont1: "Do not cut the wound or try to squeeze out blood or venom.",
      dont2: "Do not suck out the venom with your mouth or any tool.",
      dont3: "Do not bind the limb with a tourniquet; it may increase the damage and endanger the limb.",
      dont4: "Do not apply ice or cauterize the bite site.",
      dont5: "Do not drink alcohol or stimulants like coffee, and do not take painkillers without medical supervision.",
      dont6: "Do not try to catch or kill the snake to bring it; noting its appearance from a distance, or a photo if safely possible, is enough.",
      s4title: "Signs that require urgent care",
      s4: "Some bites are 'dry,' without venom, but this cannot be determined in the field, so every bite is treated as a medical case. Watch for severe pain or increasing swelling around the bite, bleeding, dizziness and nausea, difficulty breathing, or numbness — all of which require quickly reaching hospital. Do not wait for symptoms to worsen before seeking help.",
      s5title: "If your pet is bitten",
      s5: "Snakebites can be fatal to dogs and cats and need prompt care. Go to a veterinarian immediately, try to keep the animal calm and still during transport, and do not try to treat it yourself or give it human medicines. If possible, note the snake's appearance from a distance to help the vet without putting yourself at risk.",
      s6title: "At the hospital and after",
      s6: "At the hospital, doctors assess the situation and give antivenom when needed under monitoring, along with treating pain and swelling. Most victims recover fully with proper treatment. After recovery, think about prevention: watch where you place your feet and hands in nature, use a flashlight at night, and keep away from your home what attracts snakes, such as rodents and woodpiles.",
      close: "Always remember: this guide is for awareness and is not a substitute for medical care. In any bite, the absolute priority is to call emergency services and reach hospital quickly. Calm and knowledge are your first line of defense.",
    },
