    seasonGuide: {
      breadcrumb: "When Are Snakes Active",
      eyebrow: "Seasonal Guide",
      title: "When Are Snakes Active? A Month-by-Month Guide to Their Seasons",
      lead: "Why do we see snakes in one season and barely glimpse them in another? A comprehensive guide to snake activity seasons in Israel throughout the year, and how to protect yourself in each season.",
      introA: "One of the most common questions we receive is: 'When do snakes come out?' and 'Should I worry at this time of year?'. The answer is not a specific day on the calendar, but a story governed by heat above all else. Snakes, being cold-blooded creatures, do not live by the clock as we do, but by the rhythm of the sun and the seasons.",
      introB: "Understanding this seasonal rhythm is not merely scientific curiosity, but a practical safety tool: if you know when and where snakes are likely to be active, you know when to raise your caution and when to relax. In this guide we take you through the whole year, season by season and month by month, so you understand snake behavior at every stage.",
      s1title: "Why does the weather govern snake activity?",
      s1a: "Snakes are 'cold-blooded' creatures, or more precisely: creatures that depend on external heat to regulate their body temperature. They do not generate internal heat like mammals, but draw it from the sun, rocks, and ground. This makes the temperature of their surroundings the primary controller of everything they do: when to move, when to hunt, and when to hide.",
      s1b: "When the weather is very cold, the snake becomes slow, sluggish, and unable to hunt or defend itself, so it turns to hiding and stillness. When heat rises above a certain threshold, coming out by day becomes a lethal danger to it, so it switches to nocturnal activity. Between these two extremes lies the 'optimal temperature zone' in which snakes are active, searching for food and a mate.",
      s1c: "So one cannot say that snakes 'come out in a certain month' absolutely; it changes by region (cold north or hot desert), and by the weather in that specific year. But there is a general pattern that recurs every year, which we'll detail now.",
      s2title: "Spring: the peak season and the greatest danger",
      s2a: "Spring (roughly March to May) is the most active season for snakes, and the period in which most encounters and bites occur. The reason is simple: after the months of winter dormancy, snakes emerge hungry to bask and replenish their energy, and this coincides with the mating season that drives them to move in search of a partner.",
      s2b: "In this season the heat is mild and optimal, so snakes are clearly active by day — unlike summer. You see them basking on rocks, trails, and walls in the morning and afternoon. This period also coincides with people going out into nature, on trips, and working in gardens, so the chance of an encounter increases on both sides.",
      s2c: "So spring is the season that requires the utmost caution: watch where you place your feet and hands in nature, especially near rocks, tall grass, and woodpiles. If you are working in your garden, wear closed shoes and gloves, and check the area before reaching in with your hand.",
      s3title: "Summer: activity that hides in the night",
      s3a: "In summer (roughly June to August), snakes do not disappear, but they change their schedule. The peak of the day's heat becomes lethal to them, so the activity of many species — especially desert ones — shifts to dusk and night, when the heat moderates. By day they hide under rocks and in cool burrows and cracks.",
      s3b: "This means the daytime danger in summer is relatively lower, but the nighttime danger rises. Walking at night in desert or natural areas without light becomes risky, because you may not see an active snake in front of you. The golden summer rule: use a strong flashlight for any night movement in nature, and watch your steps.",
      s3c: "In the cooler northern and Mediterranean regions, some snakes may remain active in the early morning and evening hours too. Near homes, summer is a season when snakes look for water and shade, so they may approach gardens, pools, and water sources.",
      s4title: "Autumn: a second wave of activity",
      s4a: "Autumn (roughly September to November) resembles spring in being a transition season, and the heat returns to moderation after the harshness of summer. So autumn sees a **second wave of activity**: snakes return to daytime activity, and many of them prepare for winter with intensive feeding to store energy.",
      s4b: "In this season, the eggs of many species laid in summer also hatch, so baby snakes appear. Despite their small size, the young of venomous species carry effective venom, and must be treated with the same caution. Do not underestimate a small snake just because of its size.",
      s4c: "So daytime caution in autumn is as important as in spring, especially on warm, sunny days when snakes come out to bask before the cold sets in.",
      s5title: "Winter: the season of dormancy and rest",
      s5a: "Winter (roughly December to February) is the quietest season. As the temperature drops, snakes enter a state of seasonal dormancy (similar to hibernation but not a deep sleep like that of mammals), withdrawing into their burrows, rock crevices, and underground, and reducing their movement to a minimum to conserve energy.",
      s5b: "So the chances of meeting a snake in winter are the lowest of the year. But this does not mean a total absence of danger: on exceptionally sunny, warm winter days, some snakes may come out for a few hours to bask and then return. Also, a snake hiding in a warm place may be startled if you move woodpiles or stones.",
      s5c: "Winter is a good time for proactive prevention: check the surroundings of your home, seal cracks and openings, and clear woodpiles and stone piles that snakes might adopt as shelter before the coming spring.",
      s6title: "A brief table by season",
      s6a: "In practical brief: **Spring** peak activity and danger — utmost caution by day. **Summer** mostly nocturnal activity — beware when walking at night and use a flashlight. **Autumn** a second wave of activity and the appearance of young — renewed daytime caution. **Winter** dormancy and rest — less dangerous, and a time for proactive prevention.",
      s6b: "But always remember: these are general patterns influenced by region and weather. The desert differs from the Galilee, and a warm year differs from a cold one. The rule that does not change with the seasons: respect the snake, don't approach it, and don't try to kill or catch it at any time of year.",
      close: "Knowing the seasons of snake activity transforms you from someone who fears the unknown into someone who understands and prepares. Snakes are part of our nature throughout the year, and each season has its rhythm. With caution in the active seasons and prevention in the quiet ones, you can live and hike safely side by side with these creatures. And in any encounter or bite, the rule is constant in every season: move away, don't touch, and seek help immediately.",
    },
