      lead: "At their summer activity peak, snakes are seen more often near residential areas — natural behavior that doesn't mean they seek people.",
      intro: "During the summer months, rising temperatures across Israel have a direct impact on the behavior of many wildlife species, including snakes. This period represents the peak season of activity for most snake species, increasing the likelihood of encounters in natural habitats, agricultural areas, and occasionally near residential neighborhoods. It is important to emphasize that the presence of snakes near people does not indicate that they are seeking human contact or attempting to attack. Rather, it reflects their natural ecological behavior.",
      nightTitle: "Why are they active at night in summer?",
      nightText: "Snakes are ectothermic animals, meaning they rely on environmental temperatures to regulate their body functions. During the hot summer months, daytime ground temperatures often become excessively high, causing most snake species in Israel to be active primarily during the evening, nighttime, and early morning hours, when temperatures are more favorable. During these periods, they search for food, water, and suitable shelter.",
      nearTitle: "Why do they come near homes?",
      nearText: "Residential areas may unintentionally provide favorable habitats for snakes. Irrigated gardens, dense vegetation, piles of wood or stones, storage areas, and abandoned structures offer shelter, while rodents, lizards, and amphibians provide a natural food source. Consequently, snakes are attracted to the availability of prey and suitable habitat rather than to human activity.",
